---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import ArticleImage from "../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro";
import type { FaqItem } from "../../lib/seo";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  { q: "How many calories should a woman eat daily?", a: "Most women need 1,600–2,400 calories daily depending on age, height, weight, and activity. Use our calorie calculator for your exact number." },
  { q: "How many calories should a man eat daily?", a: "Most men need 2,200–3,200 calories daily depending on size and activity level. Our calculator finds your precise target based on your stats." },
  { q: "What is the minimum calorie intake for women?", a: "Generally, women should not drop below 1,200 calories daily without medical supervision. This protects muscle and metabolic health." },
  { q: "What is the minimum calorie intake for men?", a: "Men should typically not go below 1,500 calories daily without supervision. Lower intakes risk muscle loss and metabolic slowdown." },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Calorie Calculator by Age and Sex — Daily Calorie Needs Guide"
  h1="Calorie Calculator by Age and Sex"
  description="Calorie calculator by age and sex. Find daily calorie needs for women, men, and by decade. Includes recommended ranges and minimums."
  path="/guides/calorie-calculator-by-age/"
  category="Guides"
  categoryPath="/guides/"
  lead="Daily calorie needs vary significantly by age, sex, and body size. Find your exact number."
  updated="June 2026"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>Your calorie needs change throughout life. This <strong>calorie calculator by age and sex</strong> guide shows how factors affect your <a href="/guides/maintenance-calorie-calculator/">maintenance calories</a>.</p>

  <h2>Daily calorie needs by age and sex (statistics)</h2>

  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/birthday-cake.svg" alt="Illustration of a birthday cake with candles representing age-based calorie calculation." />
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Age Group</th><th>Women (sedentary)</th><th>Women (active)</th><th>Men (sedentary)</th><th>Men (active)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>18–25 years</td><td>1,800–2,200</td><td>2,200–2,600</td><td>2,400–2,800</td><td>2,800–3,200</td></tr>
      <tr><td>26–40 years</td><td>1,800–2,400</td><td>2,200–2,800</td><td>2,200–3,000</td><td>2,800–3,400</td></tr>
      <tr><td>41–55 years</td><td>1,600–2,200</td><td>2,000–2,600</td><td>2,200–2,800</td><td>2,600–3,200</td></tr>
      <tr><td>56+ years</td><td>1,400–2,000</td><td>1,800–2,200</td><td>2,000–2,400</td><td>2,400–2,800</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How age affects calorie needs</h2>
  <p>Metabolism slows gradually after age 25. On average:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>1–2% slower per decade due to muscle loss and hormonal changes</li>
    <li>~100–200 fewer calories needed by age 50</li>
    <li>~200–400 fewer by age 65 compared to age 25</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This is why a <a href="/guides/online-calorie-calculator/">calorie calculator</a> should be updated periodically.</p>

  <h2>How sex affects calorie needs</h2>
  <p>Men typically need more calories because they have:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Higher muscle mass on average (muscle burns more calories)</li>
    <li>Larger organ size and body surface area</li>
    <li>Different hormonal profiles affecting metabolism</li>
  </ul>
  <p>However, individual variation is huge. Always calculate based on your stats.</p>

  <h2>Minimum recommended intakes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Minimum daily</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Women</td><td>1,200 calories</td><td>Protects muscle, supports basic metabolic functions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Men</td><td>1,500 calories</td><td>Similar protection for male physiology</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Children/teenagers</td><td>Age-specific (1,600+)</td><td>Growth and development requirements</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Calorie calculator accuracy for different groups</h2>
  <p>Research shows metabolic equations vary in accuracy:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Young adults: highest accuracy (90%+)</li>
    <li>Middle-aged: good accuracy (85–90%)</li>
    <li>Older adults: moderate accuracy (75–85%)</li>
    <li>Obese individuals: formulas often overestimate (need adjustment)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Personalized calculation beats averages</h2>
  <p>While averages provide context, your exact needs depend on:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Current weight and body composition</li>
    <li>Muscle mass percentage</li>
    <li>Daily activity outside formal exercise</li>
    <li>Health conditions and medications</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use our <a href="/">calorie calculator</a> for your personalized number, then adjust based on real-world results.</p>

  <h2>Adjusting for body size</h2>
  <p>Heavier individuals burn more calories. Rough estimates:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each additional kg adds ~10–20 calories to maintenance</li>
    <li>Each additional lb adds ~20–40 calories</li>
    <li>Muscle burns ~15–25% more than fat tissue</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This is why using your actual weight in the <a href="/bmr-calculator/">BMR calculator</a> matters.</p>
</ContentLayout>
